1
00:00:00,318 --> 00:00:01,773
I want to give away Bitcoin.

2
00:00:02,064 --> 00:00:03,100
The prize is ready,

3
00:00:03,282 --> 00:00:04,736
but I don’t know the winner yet.

4
00:00:04,973 --> 00:00:06,227
Or their Bitcoin address.

5
00:00:06,955 --> 00:00:08,591
That’s why I built PrizeKey.

6
00:00:08,755 --> 00:00:09,991
Set up the gift first,

7
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,918
pass its claim on NEAR to the winner,

8
00:00:12,155 --> 00:00:13,991
and let them choose their Bitcoin address.

9
00:00:14,227 --> 00:00:15,000
Let me show you.

10
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:16,773
In this earlier test,

11
00:00:17,027 --> 00:00:19,573
I funded a gift with 20,000 test sats.

12
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:21,591
It has its own Bitcoin address.

13
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,955
Once I have a winner,

14
00:00:24,055 --> 00:00:25,164
I enter their NEAR account

15
00:00:25,164 --> 00:00:26,473
and pass them the claim.

16
00:00:26,636 --> 00:00:28,318
I don’t need their Bitcoin address.

17
00:00:30,227 --> 00:00:31,591
Now they’re the holder.

18
00:00:31,682 --> 00:00:33,609
They choose where to receive the Bitcoin,

19
00:00:33,609 --> 00:00:35,227
and approve the payout.

20
00:00:37,273 --> 00:00:39,455
NEAR Chain Signatures authorizes it,

21
00:00:39,691 --> 00:00:41,318
then the browser sends Bitcoin.

22
00:00:44,773 --> 00:00:46,409
Here’s what the recipient sees today.

23
00:00:46,627 --> 00:00:49,591
19,750 test sats delivered,

24
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:51,955
after a 250-sat fee.

25
00:00:53,909 --> 00:00:54,864
If they want to check,

26
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,818
the transaction is right here.

27
00:00:59,182 --> 00:01:00,309
Fund the prize first.

28
00:01:00,455 --> 00:01:02,218
NEAR tracks who owns the claim.

29
00:01:02,473 --> 00:01:04,073
Chain Signatures lets that person

30
00:01:04,073 --> 00:01:05,964
approve a native Bitcoin payout.

31
00:01:06,164 --> 00:01:07,727
The winner chooses where it goes.
